    # Dialogue 15: The Mutable Default Argument Trap
    [
        ("I have a bug in my Python code that is driving me crazy. Look at this function:\n```python\ndef add_item(item, shopping_cart=[]):\n    shopping_cart.append(item)\n    return shopping_cart\n```\nWhen I call `add_item('apple')` and then `add_item('banana')`, the second call returns `['apple', 'banana']` even though I didn't pass a cart! Why is it doing this?",
         "This is one of Python's most famous gotchas: mutable default arguments! In Python, default parameter expressions are evaluated once when the function is defined (`def`), not every time the function is called. The list `[]` is created in memory at definition time and bound to the function's internal `__defaults__` tuple. Every subsequent call that omits `shopping_cart` modifies that very same persistent list in memory."),
        ("How do I fix this properly according to Python best practices?",
         "The canonical Python pattern is to use `None` as a sentinel default value, and instantiate a new list inside the function body if none was provided:\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Optional, List\n\ndef add_item(item: str, shopping_cart: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> List[str]:\n    if shopping_cart is None:\n        shopping_cart = []\n    shopping_cart.append(item)\n    return shopping_cart\n```\nNow, whenever a caller omits `shopping_cart`, a fresh, independent list is allocated in local function scope."),
        ("Does this apply to other mutable types as well?",
         "Yes, exactly the same issue occurs with dictionaries (`{}`), sets (`set()`), and custom object instances. Always use immutable defaults—like `None`, numbers, strings, or tuples—for default argument values in Python.")
    ],

    # Dialogue 16: Dynamic Programming - Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)
    [
        ("How do I find the Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) between two strings using dynamic programming?",
         "A subsequence is a sequence derived by deleting zero or more characters without changing the relative order of the remaining characters. Let `dp[i][j]` represent the length of the LCS of `s1[0..i-1]` and `s2[0..j-1]`.\n\nThe recurrence relation is:\n1. Base case: `dp[i][0] = 0` and `dp[0][j] = 0`.\n2. If characters match (`s1[i-1] == s2[j-1]`): `dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i-1][j-1]`.\n3. If they do not match: `dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1])`.\n\nHere is the bottom-up 2D tabulation in Python:\n\n```python\ndef lcs_length(s1: str, s2: str) -> int:\n    m, n = len(s1), len(s2)\n    dp = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(m + 1)]\n\n    for i in range(1, m + 1):\n        for j in range(1, n + 1):\n            if s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]:\n                dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i - 1][j - 1]\n            else:\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])\n\n    return dp[m][n]\n```\nTime complexity is O(m * n) and space complexity is O(m * n)."),
        ("Can we optimize the space complexity if we only need the length of the LCS?",
         "Yes! Notice that computing row `i` only requires values from row `i - 1`. Therefore, we can reduce space from O(m * n) to O(min(m, n)) using a rolling array of two rows, or even a single 1D array with a temporary variable to hold the top-left diagonal:\n\n```python\ndef lcs_space_optimized(s1: str, s2: str) -> int:\n    # Ensure s2 is the shorter string to minimize space\n    if len(s1) < len(s2):\n        s1, s2 = s2, s1\n    m, n = len(s1), len(s2)\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n\n    for i in range(1, m + 1):\n        prev_diag = 0\n        for j in range(1, n + 1):\n            temp = dp[j]\n            if s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]:\n                dp[j] = 1 + prev_diag\n            else:\n                dp[j] = max(dp[j], dp[j - 1])\n            prev_diag = temp\n    return dp[n]\n```\nThis requires only O(n) memory while preserving identical O(m * n) execution time."),
        ("What if we also need to reconstruct the actual string of the subsequence?",
         "To reconstruct the actual string, we retain the 2D table and backtrack from `(m, n)` back to `(0, 0)`. If `s1[i-1] == s2[j-1]`, we include that character and move diagonally to `(i-1, j-1)`. Otherwise, we step toward whichever neighboring cell held the maximum value (`dp[i-1][j]` or `dp[i][j-1]`). Finally, reversing the collected characters yields the exact LCS string.")
    ],

    # Dialogue 17: Dynamic Programming - 0/1 Knapsack
    [
        ("How does the 0/1 Knapsack problem work, and how do we solve it with dynamic programming?",
         "In the 0/1 Knapsack problem, you are given `N` items, each with a weight `w_i` and value `v_i`, and a knapsack with maximum weight capacity `W`. You must choose a subset of items to maximize total value without exceeding capacity `W`. Each item can be included at most once (0 or 1).\n\nLet `dp[i][w]` be the maximum value attainable using a subset of the first `i` items with a weight capacity `w`. For item `i`:\n- If `weights[i-1] > w`, we cannot take it: `dp[i][w] = dp[i-1][w]`.\n- Otherwise: `dp[i][w] = max(dp[i-1][w], values[i-1] + dp[i-1][w - weights[i-1]])`.\n\nHere is the bottom-up solution:\n\n```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef knapsack_01(weights: List[int], values: List[int], capacity: int) -> int:\n    n = len(weights)\n    dp = [[0] * (capacity + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        w_i = weights[i - 1]\n        v_i = values[i - 1]\n        for w in range(capacity + 1):\n            if w_i <= w:\n                dp[i][w] = max(dp[i - 1][w], v_i + dp[i - 1][w - w_i])\n            else:\n                dp[i][w] = dp[i - 1][w]\n\n    return dp[n][capacity]\n```"),
        ("How can we find the exact items chosen rather than just the final value?",
         "We trace backward from `dp[n][capacity]`:\n\n```python\ndef get_knapsack_items(weights, values, capacity, dp):\n    chosen_indices = []\n    w = capacity\n    for i in range(len(weights), 0, -1):\n        # If value differs from the row above, this item was included\n        if dp[i][w] != dp[i - 1][w]:\n            chosen_indices.append(i - 1)\n            w -= weights[i - 1]\n    chosen_indices.reverse()\n    return chosen_indices\n```\nIf `dp[i][w] == dp[i-1][w]`, item `i-1` was skipped. If different, it was included, so we record its index and subtract its weight before moving up to row `i-1`.")
    ],

    # Dialogue 18: Graph Algorithms - Dijkstra's Shortest Path
    [
        ("Can you show me how to implement Dijkstra's algorithm cleanly in Python using a min-heap?",
         "Certainly! Dijkstra's algorithm finds the shortest path from a single source node to all other nodes in a weighted graph with non-negative edge weights. Using Python's standard `heapq` module gives an efficient O((V + E) log V) time complexity:\n\n```python\nimport heapq\nfrom typing import Dict, List, Tuple\n\ndef dijkstra(graph: Dict[str, List[Tuple[str, float]]], start_node: str) -> Dict[str, float]:\n    # graph format: {'A': [('B', weight), ('C', weight)]}\n    distances = {node: float('inf') for node in graph}\n    distances[start_node] = 0.0\n    priority_queue = [(0.0, start_node)]  # (distance, node)\n\n    while priority_queue:\n        curr_dist, curr_node = heapq.heappop(priority_queue)\n\n        # Skip stale entries if we already found a shorter path\n        if curr_dist > distances[curr_node]:\n            continue\n\n        for neighbor, weight in graph.get(curr_node, []):\n            distance = curr_dist + weight\n            if distance < distances[neighbor]:\n                distances[neighbor] = distance\n                heapq.heappush(priority_queue, (distance, neighbor))\n\n    return distances\n```"),
        ("Why does Dijkstra's algorithm fail if a graph contains negative edge weights?",
         "Dijkstra relies on a greedy invariant: once a node is popped from the min-heap with distance `d`, that distance is guaranteed to be optimal because all future explored edges will only add non-negative costs. If negative edge weights exist, a longer path with many edges could suddenly decrease its cumulative cost below `d`, violating the invariant.\n\nFurthermore, if there is a negative cycle, costs can decrease indefinitely to negative infinity."),
        ("What algorithm should we use if negative weights are present?",
         "You should use the Bellman-Ford algorithm. It relaxes all edges `|V| - 1` times and has an O(V * E) time complexity. On the `|V|`-th iteration, if any edge can still be relaxed, Bellman-Ford flags the existence of a negative cycle.")
    ],

    # Dialogue 19: Robust Binary Search & Lower Bound
    [
        ("Binary search seems simple, but people often get off-by-one errors. How do I write a foolproof binary search in Python?",
         "The secret to foolproof binary search is maintaining strict loop invariants. Here is the canonical standard binary search:\n\n```python\nfrom typing import List, Optional\n\ndef binary_search(nums: List[int], target: int) -> Optional[int]:\n    low = 0\n    high = len(nums) - 1\n\n    while low <= high:\n        # Prevent potential integer overflow in other languages: low + (high - low) // 2\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if nums[mid] == target:\n            return mid\n        elif nums[mid] < target:\n            low = mid + 1\n        else:\n            high = mid - 1\n\n    return None\n```\nKey invariant: At the start of each iteration, if `target` exists, it must lie within the closed interval `[low, high]`."),
        ("How do I modify this to find the `lower_bound`—the first element greater than or equal to `target`?",
         "To find `lower_bound` (equivalent to `bisect.bisect_left`), we maintain the half-open interval `[low, high)` where `high = len(nums)`:\n\n```python\ndef lower_bound(nums: List[int], target: int) -> int:\n    low = 0\n    high = len(nums)\n    while low < high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if nums[mid] < target:\n            low = mid + 1\n        else:\n            high = mid\n    return low\n```\nWhen the loop terminates, `low == high`, pointing precisely to the first index where `nums[index] >= target` (or `len(nums)` if all elements are smaller).")
    ],

    # Dialogue 20: Trie (Prefix Tree) Implementation
    [
        ("How do I build a Trie data structure in Python for fast prefix search and autocomplete?",
         "A Trie (prefix tree) stores strings as a tree where each node represents a character, allowing O(L) insertion and search where L is string length:\n\n```python\nclass TrieNode:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.children = {}\n        self.is_end_of_word = False\n\nclass Trie:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.root = TrieNode()\n\n    def insert(self, word: str) -> None:\n        curr = self.root\n        for char in word:\n            if char not in curr.children:\n                curr.children[char] = TrieNode()\n            curr = curr.children[char]\n        curr.is_end_of_word = True\n\n    def search(self, word: str) -> bool:\n        curr = self.root\n        for char in word:\n            if char not in curr.children:\n                return False\n            curr = curr.children[char]\n        return curr.is_end_of_word\n```"),
        ("How do we implement prefix auto-completion to return all words starting with a given prefix?",
         "We first navigate down to the prefix's ending node, then execute a depth-first traversal to collect all valid word completions:\n\n```python\n    def autocomplete(self, prefix: str) -> list[str]:\n        curr = self.root\n        for char in prefix:\n            if char not in curr.children:\n                return []\n            curr = curr.children[char]\n\n        results = []\n        def dfs(node: TrieNode, path: list[str]):\n            if node.is_end_of_word:\n                results.append(\"\".join(path))\n            for char, child_node in node.children.items():\n                path.append(char)\n                dfs(child_node, path)\n                path.pop()\n\n        dfs(curr, list(prefix))\n        return results\n```\nThis efficiently locates candidate completions in O(P + K) time, where P is prefix length and K is the total characters in matched subtree paths.")
    ],

